insert into public.devotionals (n, title, body, today, pray) values
(0, $t$Here am I$t$, $t$Isaiah had just seen the Lord high and lifted up. He'd been undone by his own sin and then cleansed by God's mercy. Only after that did God ask, "Whom shall I send?"

Isaiah's "send me" wasn't bravado. It was the response of someone who had been forgiven. When you know how much grace you've received, you want others to know it too.

God still asks the same question. He isn't looking for the most gifted person in the room. He's looking for someone willing. The person He sends you to might live across the street or sit across the breakroom.

You don't have to know where it leads. Just say yes today.$t$, $t$Ask God, "Who are You sending me to this week?" Add whoever comes to mind.$t$, $t$Here am I, Lord. Send me.$t$),
(1, $t$Not ashamed$t$, $t$Paul had been mocked, jailed, and run out of cities for the gospel. Still he said, "I am not ashamed."

Why? Because he knew what the gospel is: the power of God. It isn't just good advice or one opinion among many. It's the message God uses to rescue people.

We sometimes soften the message or avoid it because we're worried about how it sounds. But the power isn't in how smoothly we say it. The power is in the message itself, because God is behind it.

You can be gentle, humble, and wise, and still not be ashamed. The good news about Jesus is the best news anyone will ever hear.$t$, $t$Think about how you'd explain the gospel in a few sentences. Ask God for a chance to share it this week.$t$, $t$Lord, make me confident in the power of Your gospel.$t$),
(2, $t$Let your light shine$t$, $t$Jesus doesn't say "make your light shine." He says "let" it. The light is already there because He lives in you. Our job is not to cover it up.

People notice how we live. They notice honesty at work, patience with kids, kindness to the server who got the order wrong. Good works don't replace telling people about Jesus, but they often open the door to it.

Notice where the glory goes: to your Father. The goal isn't for people to think you're a good person. It's for them to wonder about the God who makes you that way.$t$, $t$Do one quiet good work for someone you're tending, without mentioning it to anyone.$t$, $t$Father, let Your light shine through me today.$t$),
(3, $t$Not a spirit of fear$t$, $t$Most of us feel some fear about talking with people about Jesus. What if they get offended? What if they ask something I can't answer? What if it changes the friendship?

Paul wrote these words to Timothy, a young preacher who seems to have struggled with timidity. Paul didn't tell him the fear wasn't real. He reminded him what God had given him instead: power, love, and a sound mind.

Love is the key. When you love someone more than you fear their reaction, you find courage you didn't know you had. Fear asks, "What will happen to me?" Love asks, "What's best for them?"

You don't have to feel brave. Take one small step while you're still nervous.$t$, $t$Name the fear that holds you back most. Give it to God, then take one small step anyway.$t$, $t$Holy Spirit, replace my fear with Your power and love.$t$),
(4, $t$In due season$t$, $t$Faithfulness gets tiring. You text, pray, invite, and show up, and sometimes nothing seems to change.

Paul knew that feeling. That's why he wrote this. He doesn't promise a quick harvest. He promises a sure one: "in due season." God has a timetable, and it's usually longer than ours.

The condition is simple: don't give up. Farmers don't dig up seeds to check on them. They keep watering and wait.

If you're weary today, that's not failure. It's a sign you've been working. Rest in God, and then keep going.$t$, $t$Look at someone you've been tending for a long time. Take one more small step with them.$t$, $t$Lord, renew my strength. Help me not give up.$t$),
(5, $t$How shall they hear?$t$, $t$Paul walks backward through a chain. To call on Jesus, people must believe. To believe, they must hear. To hear, someone has to tell them.

"Preacher" here doesn't only mean pastors. It means anyone who tells the good news. A friend over coffee. A parent at bedtime. A coworker on a long drive.

God could have chosen angels or skywriting. Instead, He chose people like you. Someone in your life may never hear the gospel clearly unless you're the one who says it.

That's not meant to weigh you down. It's an honor.$t$, $t$Think of someone who may have never heard the gospel clearly. Pray for a chance to tell them.$t$, $t$Lord, let me be the one who tells them.$t$),
(6, $t$You shall receive power$t$, $t$Jesus didn't tell His disciples to work up the courage to be witnesses. He told them they would receive power from the Holy Spirit.

Notice the order of places. It starts with Jerusalem, their home city. Then the surrounding region, then Samaria, the people they didn't get along with, and then the whole world.

Your Jerusalem is your home, street, and workplace. Your Samaria might be the person you'd rather avoid. The Spirit's power reaches all of it.

You don't witness in your own strength. Ask for His.$t$, $t$Name your "Jerusalem" and your "Samaria." Pray for one person in each.$t$, $t$Holy Spirit, fill me with power to be a witness today.$t$),
(7, $t$For God so loved$t$, $t$It may be the most familiar verse in the Bible, and it's easy to read past it. But read it slowly with one person in mind.

"God so loved" your neighbor. He gave His Son for your coworker. "Whosoever" includes the friend you think is too far gone.

This verse is the whole gospel in one sentence: God's love, His gift, our need to believe, and the life He offers. If you ever freeze up and don't know what to say, this is a good place to start.

The word "whosoever" leaves no one out.$t$, $t$Read John 3:16 again, putting the name of someone you're tending in place of "the world."$t$, $t$Father, thank You for loving the people I love even more than I do.$t$),
(8, $t$Seek and save$t$, $t$Jesus said this right after eating at Zacchaeus's house. The crowd grumbled that He had gone to a sinner's home. Jesus answered with His mission statement.

He came to seek. He didn't wait for lost people to find their way to Him. He went to them, called them by name, and sat at their tables.

If Jesus seeks, His followers seek too. That means going where people are instead of waiting for them to come to church.

No one is too lost for Jesus. Being lost is exactly what qualifies them.$t$, $t$Go where someone you're tending already is: their game, their shop, their table. Set a Meetup.$t$, $t$Jesus, make me a seeker like You.$t$),
(9, $t$God gives the growth$t$, $t$Paul planted. Apollos watered. Neither of them made anything grow. That was God's work.

This is good news for anyone who feels responsible for someone's salvation. You aren't. You might be the one who plants a first seed of curiosity. You might water what someone else planted years ago. You might never see the harvest at all.

Every part matters, and none of it depends on you alone. A kind word, an invitation, a prayer, an answered question: these are seeds and water. God takes them and does what only He can do.

So be faithful with your part today, and trust Him with the rest.$t$, $t$Take one small step with someone, like a text, a prayer, or a note. Then leave the results with God.$t$, $t$Lord, I'll plant and water. Please give the growth.$t$),
(10, $t$Seasoned with salt$t$, $t$Salt brings out flavor and makes people thirsty. Gracious speech does the same thing. It makes people want to keep talking.

Paul says "alway," not just during spiritual conversations. The way you talk about your boss, respond to a frustrating text, or joke with friends is all part of your witness. People listen to how we talk long before they ask what we believe.

Notice also "every man." Each person is different. One friend needs to be heard. Another needs a straight answer. Another needs a laugh before anything serious. Grace pays attention to the person in front of you.$t$, $t$Before your next conversation with someone you're tending, look at their notes in Tend. Remember what they care about.$t$, $t$Lord, let my words today be full of grace.$t$),
(11, $t$Not in vain$t$, $t$This verse comes at the end of Paul's great chapter on the resurrection. Because Jesus rose, nothing done for Him is wasted.

That changes how we see small things. The prayer that seemed unanswered. The conversation that went nowhere. The invitation that got turned down. None of it is in vain.

Paul says to be "stedfast" and "unmoveable," steady when nothing seems to be happening. Faithfulness is often unseen, but God sees all of it.$t$, $t$Write down one thing you did for someone this week that felt like it didn't matter. Thank God that it did.$t$, $t$Lord, keep me steady. My work for You is not in vain.$t$),
(12, $t$Ambassadors$t$, $t$An ambassador represents a king in a foreign land. They don't speak for themselves. They carry the king's message.

Paul says that's what we are. God makes His appeal to people through us. When you speak about Jesus, God Himself is pleading through your words: "Be reconciled to Me."

That's a high calling and a humble one. It isn't your message. You don't have to invent it or make it impressive. Your job is to carry it faithfully and kindly.

The message is reconciliation, God making peace with people through Jesus.$t$, $t$Before a conversation with someone you're tending, remind yourself: "I'm here on the King's behalf."$t$, $t$Lord, make me a faithful ambassador for You.$t$),
(13, $t$Be strong and courageous$t$, $t$Joshua was taking over from Moses and leading Israel into a land full of enemies. God didn't tell him the job would be easy. He told him He would be there.

Courage isn't the absence of fear. It's acting while trusting that God is with you. The reason Joshua could be strong was "the LORD thy God is with thee."

That promise goes with you too: into the hard conversation, the awkward invitation, the visit to the hospital. Wherever you go, He's already there.$t$, $t$What step have you been avoiding because you're nervous? Do it today, trusting God goes with you.$t$, $t$Lord, thank You that You go with me everywhere.$t$),
(14, $t$I will uphold thee$t$, $t$Count the promises in this one verse: I am with you. I am your God. I will strengthen you. I will help you. I will uphold you.

God piles them up because He knows how easily we're afraid. Afraid of rejection, of saying the wrong thing, of looking foolish in front of people we care about.

Every one of those promises holds true when you open your mouth to talk about Him. You aren't holding yourself up. His right hand is holding you.

When fear rises today, answer it with one of these promises. Say it out loud if you need to.$t$, $t$Pick the promise from this verse you need most. Carry it into your next conversation.$t$, $t$Lord, hold me up today. I'm leaning on You.$t$),
(15, $t$It shall not return void$t$, $t$Just before this verse, God compares His word to rain and snow. They come down, soak into the ground, and make things grow. They don't fall for nothing.

God's word works the same way. When you share a verse with a friend, it doesn't just bounce off. It goes in and does what God sends it to do, even if you never see the result.

That's why sending a verse is never a small thing. You're not passing along a nice saying. You're planting God's living word in someone's life.$t$, $t$Send a verse to someone you're tending, with a short note about why it made you think of them.$t$, $t$Lord, let Your word do its work in the people I love.$t$),
(16, $t$He that winneth souls$t$, $t$The world measures wisdom by degrees, income, and influence. Proverbs measures it differently. The wise person is the one who wins souls.

"Winning" souls isn't about winning arguments. It's about winning people: their trust, their hearts, and ultimately their trust in Jesus. That takes patience, kindness, and time.

A righteous life is like a tree of life. People find shade and fruit near it. Over time, a life lived close to God draws others close too.

Of all the things you could be wise about, this one lasts forever.$t$, $t$Ask yourself, "Am I building trust with the people I'm tending?" Do one thing that builds it.$t$, $t$Lord, make me wise in the ways that matter forever.$t$),
(17, $t$As the stars$t$, $t$Daniel looks ahead to the end of time. Those who turn others toward God will shine like stars forever.

Most faithful witness is unseen here. No one applauds a quiet prayer or a patient friendship. But God sees, and He promises that it will shine for eternity.

Think of the people who turned you toward God. A parent, a pastor, a friend who kept inviting you. In God's eyes, they shine.

You get to be that for someone else.$t$, $t$Thank God for the person who first pointed you to Jesus. If you can, send them a note telling them.$t$, $t$Lord, use my life to turn someone toward You.$t$),
(18, $t$Declare His glory$t$, $t$This psalm is a song, and this verse is a call to tell everyone what God has done.

We naturally tell people about things that excite us: a great restaurant, a game, a new baby. The psalmist says God's wonders deserve the same.

What has God done in your life lately? An answered prayer. Peace in a hard week. Provision you didn't expect. These are wonders worth telling. They don't have to be dramatic to be real.

Talking about God naturally, the same way you'd talk about anything good, makes faith feel normal to the people around you.$t$, $t$Share one thing God has done for you recently with someone, in normal conversation.$t$, $t$Lord, let me speak about You as naturally as I speak about anything I love.$t$),
(19, $t$Sow in tears$t$, $t$Some sowing is done with tears. Praying for a child who has walked away. Loving a spouse who doesn't believe. Caring for a friend who keeps pushing God off.

Those tears aren't wasted. The psalmist promises that tears in the planting season lead to joy in the harvest.

If you're carrying heavy grief over someone you love, bring it to God. He doesn't ask you to hide it. He collects every tear and turns them into seeds.

Keep sowing, even through the tears. Joy is coming.$t$, $t$Bring the person who causes you the most heartache to God today. Be honest with Him about how you feel.$t$, $t$Lord, take my tears and turn them into a harvest of joy.$t$),
(20, $t$Joy in heaven$t$, $t$Jesus told the story of a shepherd who left ninety-nine sheep to find one that was lost. When he found it, he carried it home on his shoulders and threw a party.

Then Jesus said heaven does the same thing. When one person turns to God, heaven celebrates.

That tells you how much God cares about the one person you're praying for. They aren't a number. Heaven is waiting to rejoice over them.

When you feel like one person doesn't matter much, remember the party.$t$, $t$Pray for one person by name, picturing heaven rejoicing on the day they come home.$t$, $t$Father, I long to see heaven rejoice over the people I love.$t$),
(21, $t$Lift up your eyes$t$, $t$The disciples had just come back from buying lunch in a Samaritan town. They walked right through a harvest field and came back with bread. Meanwhile, Jesus had been talking with one woman at a well, and soon the whole town was coming out to meet Him.

It's easy to think the harvest is someday. Someday, when I know more. Someday, when they seem more interested. Jesus says the fields are ready now.

The people in your life aren't a project for later. Someone you'll talk to this week is already wondering about God. They may not say it out loud, but they're closer than you think.

Lift up your eyes. Look at the people right in front of you.$t$, $t$Open your Focus 5 and ask, "Who here might be ready for a deeper conversation?"$t$, $t$Lord, open my eyes to the harvest You've already put around me.$t$),
(22, $t$Into all the world$t$, $t$"All the world" can feel overwhelming. We think of missionaries in faraway countries and wonder how we fit.

But your world is part of all the world. The people on your street, in your office, and on your kids' teams are part of "every creature." Most of them won't ever hear from a missionary. They'll hear from you.

Jesus' command starts with "go." The gospel moves when we move toward people. It rarely travels by waiting.

Go into your world today. It's the part of all the world God gave you.$t$, $t$List the places you'll go this week. Pray for one person you'll see in each.$t$, $t$Lord, send me into my world with Your good news.$t$),
(23, $t$Fishers of men$t$, $t$Jesus called fishermen, and He spoke their language. He didn't ask them to become something foreign. He promised to take what they knew and give it a new purpose.

Notice the order: "Follow me" comes first. "I will make you" comes second. We don't become fishers of men by trying harder. We become them by following Jesus closely. He does the making.

Good fishermen are patient. They learn where the fish are, go back again and again, and don't quit after one empty net.

Follow Him today, and trust Him to make you into what He promised.$t$, $t$Spend a few extra minutes with Jesus before you reach out to anyone. Following comes first.$t$, $t$Jesus, I'll follow You. Make me a fisher of men.$t$),
(24, $t$I am with you alway$t$, $t$The Great Commission is a big assignment: make disciples of all nations. Jesus ends it with a promise: "I am with you alway."

The command and the promise go together. We aren't sent out alone. The One who sends us goes with us.

This verse also reminds us that the goal isn't just a decision. It's disciples, people "observing" what Jesus commanded. Tending someone doesn't end when they believe. It keeps going as they learn to follow Him.

Whoever you're walking with today, Jesus is walking with you both.$t$, $t$Is there a new believer in your life? Set a next step to help them grow, like reading a Gospel together.$t$, $t$Jesus, thank You for being with me always.$t$),
(25, $t$We cannot but speak$t$, $t$Peter and John had just been ordered by the authorities to stop speaking about Jesus. Their answer: we can't help it.

They had seen Jesus heal, die, and rise again. What they had seen was too good to keep quiet.

When we struggle to talk about Jesus, sometimes the answer isn't more technique. It's more time with Him. The more we see and hear of Him in Scripture, prayer, and life, the more naturally we'll talk about Him.

What have you seen God do? That's what you have to say.$t$, $t$Write down one thing you've seen God do this year. Look for a chance to tell someone.$t$, $t$Lord, fill me so full of You that I can't help but speak.$t$);